flfiles -- the FreeLFS file manager
===================================

    flfiles [FOLDER]

Window: back, forward, up, an editable path field and a new-folder button on
top; Places (Home, the folders named in ~/.config/user-dirs.dirs, Trash) and
Drives (System, removable and boot drives, mounts under /drives or /run/media)
on the left; an icon grid; a status bar. The folder is watched with inotify,
so changes show up on their own. Idle means no wakeups, apart from the drive
check every 3 s.

Hidden files: names with a leading dot, and names listed one per line in the
folder's own .hidden file. Ctrl-H shows them.

Keys: Enter or double-click opens (a folder opens in place, a file starts its
default app), Backspace or Alt-Up goes up, Alt-Left/Right history, Ctrl-L the
path field, F2 rename, Del moves to the Trash, Shift-Del deletes for good
(asks first), Ctrl-C / Ctrl-X / Ctrl-V copy, cut and paste (also between two
flfiles windows), Ctrl-Shift-N new folder, Ctrl-A select all, F5 refresh,
letters jump to a name, Menu key opens the context menu. Ctrl-click and
Shift-click select several.

Right click on a file or folder: Open, Open with... (every app that handles the
type, the default first), Cut, Copy, Paste, Rename, Move to Trash, Delete
permanently, Extract here, Compress, Properties (name, type, size, modified,
permissions, location), Open terminal here (a folder item opens the terminal in
that folder). Right click on empty space: New folder, New empty file, Paste,
Open terminal here, Show hidden files; in the Trash also Empty Trash. In the
Trash: Restore, Copy, Delete for good.

Drag and drop: drag items onto a folder in the grid or a sidebar entry to move
them; hold Ctrl to copy. Drop on Trash in the sidebar to trash them.

Copy, move, trash, delete and restore run as a job in a child process with a
progress card at the bottom right and a Stop button; the window stays usable.
Stop ends after the file in progress. A name clash asks Replace, Keep both
(the copy gets a new name) or Skip, with "Do the same for the rest".

Trash follows the freedesktop spec: ~/.local/share/Trash/files with a
.trashinfo for each item in info/. Restore puts an item back where its
.trashinfo says it was. Empty Trash deletes everything in it.

Drives come from flmount (see below). Click an unmounted drive to mount it and
open it; the eject button ejects it. If flmount reports an error, the message
is shown in a dialog. Without flmount the removable drives stay hidden and
System and the /drives and /run/media mounts still show.

  flmount list                   one line per partition:
                                 DEV TAB LABEL TAB FSTYPE TAB SIZE_BYTES TAB MOUNTPOINT|- TAB removable|boot|internal
  flmount mount DEV              prints the mountpoint, exit 0
  flmount eject DEV              unmounts and powers down; errors on stderr, non-zero exit

Delete and Move to Trash first ask `flcrash --cant` about the selection: a vital
system folder (/sysflfs, /boot, ...) brings up the "You can't do that." screen,
and "Never mind" leaves everything alone.

Enter on a file starts the default app for its MIME type: the type comes from the shared MIME
database (/usr/share/mime/globs2), the app from mimeapps.list ([Default Applications], then
[Added Associations]; ~/.config/mimeapps.list wins over /etc/xdg and the shipped defaults in
/usr/share/applications/mimeapps.list: images -> Images, PDF/EPUB -> Documents, audio -> Music, video ->
Videos, text -> fledit, archives -> Archives), else the first .desktop file that lists the type.
Text files with no known name still open in fledit; a file with no app reports that.
